_irNotes: {
	"decision-01": "Every entity is tab: a gallery's state is the visit's, so nothing here emits a table, a policy, a publication or a pipeline; the durability ladder is demonstrated by the rows dying with the tab, not documented"
	"decision-02": "Behavior is data, not code: each stateful component carries a #Machine — the XState-JSON subset whose one action is writing the target state into the row's field — executed by the terminal through the same path as a Jessie reduce, so replay, tempo and the refusal event apply with the machine knowing nothing; a guard is the cliff where an app writes the reduce instead, and this gallery crosses it once, for a value and never for a decision (decision-34)"
	"decision-03": "State names are the ARIA attribute's values: the switch's states are 'true' and 'false' because aria-checked speaks that vocabulary, so one field binds the semantics and the styling hook and no component carries two spellings of one fact"
	"decision-04": "Components are CUE definitions composed into #Screen.markup at emit; the omnishell-- tag survives in the served HTML as an inert wrapper — visible to devtools, CSS and the visual battery, registered with nothing, no customElements.define and no shadow DOM"
	"decision-05": "shadcn's theming is CSS variables, so its palette lands on the design-token contract and dark mode is each token's light-dark() twin; there is no theme-switch control because appearance is a token resolution, reviewed as the storyboard's -dark frames"
	"decision-06": "No auth block: a component gallery gates nobody, and a sign-in in front of a reference is a toll"
	"decision-07": "The machine is the writer of the initial fact: the switch region carries no data-empty-row and the terminal synthesizes its fallback row from the filter's pinned id plus the machine's initial; the row-readout pane keeps a data-empty-row because it shows a fuller row than the machine's one field, and the generate-time agreement check keeps the two declarations one fact"
	"decision-08": "The combobox is deliberately last in the catalog: its virtual-focus behavior is the one part with no declarative precedent, and it is where the widget tier died the first time"
	"decision-09": "N triggers share one machine, so the discrimination is component-generated: #Tabs writes one click@trigger-<name> transition per (state, trigger) pair — a grammar the interpreter resolves and no author learns — and each transition's literal assigns keep one aria-selected column per trigger in step, so the ARIA contract stays plain bindings over the row"
	"decision-10": "The picker splits its two behaviors across the two closed vocabularies that own them: selection is a machine — one state per option, component-generated click@trigger arrows, literal assigns keeping the readout label and every aria-selected in step — and openness is the platform's popover commanded by commandfor, with anchor positioning in CSS. Light dismiss, top layer and the invoker's expanded state ship as two attributes and zero script. An option button carries both at once: its id feeds the machine, its command closes the list"
	"decision-11": "The segmented control is a radiogroup, not a bag of pressed buttons. shadcn's ToggleGroup offers both spellings, and the single-select arm is the one a #Machine already fits: the field holds exactly one state name, so the choice is single-valued, and every arrow's literal assigns project that one value onto all N chk_ columns at once — which is what leaves no arrow able to check two members, or none. That is the promise role=\"radiogroup\" with aria-checked makes and role=\"group\" with aria-pressed does not. The self-arrow is omitted as #Tabs omits it, so there is no deselect and a click on the checked option finds no candidate and writes nothing. The derived columns are chk_* because the attribute is aria-checked — an ARIA column's prefix names the attribute it binds, which is decision-03 extended one notch for the case where N triggers share one field and the field cannot itself be the ARIA value. The keyboard is APG's radio group in full: the tabstop is the chosen option's, so data-rove binds the very column aria-checked binds and one write moves the selection, the tab order and the reader's focus together — they cannot disagree because there is nothing to disagree with. The arrows come with it and never without it: a tabstop with no arrows to move it strands every unchecked option outside the tab order, which is why #OneOf draws both under one flag rather than letting a skin take half. Right and Down go to the next option, Left and Up to the previous, both WRAPPING as the pattern asks — a compile-time chart can state a wrap the read tier's lane clauses decline, because there the pattern is not known. Home and End name the ends, and the one that names the state it leaves draws no arrow, which is the rule the clicks already keep. Ids are tg-<key>-item-<name>, and neither key nor option name admits a hyphen, so the separator is unreachable from either side and two groups on one screen cannot collide; the id sits on the button and on nothing inside it, because from resolves to the nearest ancestor carrying one"
	"decision-12": "A machine's arity is one row; multiplicity is the region's. The accordion ships both of shadcn's arms from one definition to prove it rather than assert it: type=\"single\" is one region over one row whose states are the item names plus a closed none, with one exp_ column per trigger moved by literal assigns and a self-arrow targeting none — the tabs shape plus the arrow a tablist must not have. type=\"multiple\" is N sibling singleton regions on one collection, each pinning id=eq.<item>, each carrying the switch's two-state machine with its field renamed and its event narrowed: no context and no derived column, because the field IS aria-expanded's value. Nothing was added to the vocabulary for the second case, which is why #Machine never needed a plural — the arity lives in the entity's shape (one row of 1+N columns, or N rows of two, the item set moving from the field's constraint to the pk's) and in how many regions the expansion writes. And because there is no group row at arity N, only the single arm binds data-state: which item is open is the group's own fact, and no trigger's aria-expanded spells it. The keyboard is APG's required contract whole and nothing past it, at both arities: Tab walks the triggers in document order and an open panel's content after them, and Space or Enter opens the focused header, both of them native button behaviour. Up and Down between headers, and Home and End, are a second chart beside the expansion's — #OneOf walking the headers over caret and cur_*, vertical only, because a header list gives a reader no reason to press Left or Right. It has to be a second chart: both narrow their arrows to the same headers, and which header is open and which one the reader stands on are two facts one field cannot hold without becoming their product. No tabindex ships with them, and that is the pattern's contract rather than a deferral: APG keeps every header in the Tab sequence, so the caret is data-focus and a roving tabstop would take two of three headers out of a sequence the standard puts them in. Ctrl+PageUp and Ctrl+PageDown stay absent — the walk's key set is APG's own for moving through a list, and a chord is not in it"
	"decision-13": "Every accordion arrow is narrowed to click@acc-<key>-trigger-<item>, the multiple arm's included even though its region holds exactly one button: from resolves to the nearest ancestor carrying an id and the bare event type is the candidate list's fallback, so an unnarrowed click would fire from the panel a reader is clicking inside and collapse it. A bare click is safe only where the region IS the affordance, which is true of the switch and of nothing else in this catalog. The panel's collapse is then CSS keyed on the trigger's own aria-expanded, not a bound hidden: a bound boolean attribute is absent when its value interpolates empty, so binding it would need a column holding the empty string — a second spelling of what aria-expanded already says. One rule serves both arms, and a fourth item costs no stylesheet"
	"decision-14": "Dialog and tooltip earn no machine and no row. Openness, the top layer, the inert background that is also the focus trap, Esc as a close request, the focus return and the placement are all the platform's — commanded by commandfor, positioned by anchor-name — so the tier-0 claim lands as a counted absence on the screen rather than a sentence about it, and the two panes every other screen fills with a row and a chart name who holds each state instead. Modal and non-modal are one definition's two parameters because neither contains the other: modal buys the inert background and loses backdrop light dismiss, non-modal buys light dismiss and loses the inert background, and closedby, which would close that gap, is outside this tree's measured engine matrix, so nothing depends on it. What the tooltip ships is APG's disclosure, not APG's tooltip: interestfor is implemented in no engine, so a hover-opened surface cannot ship without script, and the click-opened popover — complete, correct, zero script — ships under the name tip carrying no role=\"tooltip\" and no aria-describedby rather than wearing a contract it does not meet. The CSS-only hover surface that would wear that contract fails WCAG 2.1 SC 1.4.13's Dismissable clause by construction, because CSS has no Esc, so the gallery prints the clause rather than rendering the failure for someone to copy"
	"decision-15": "Badge, card and alert are one catalog entry because they are one family — the family with no behavior — and what they contribute is not a machine but the demonstration that shadcn's variant vocabulary lands on the token contract with no new token name. The two standard panes hold that instead: The row becomes the checked claim that the screen mounts no region and no machine, and The machine becomes The tokens, the variant→token map printed as data, because for a component whose whole content is presentation that map is the machine — and each row carries the selector chain it describes, so the suite folds the screen's stylesheet through the table and refuses a cell the rules do not set, a declaration no cell lists, or a variant on the screen with no row. It names tokens and never values — a hex printed on the screen would be the palette's second declaration, which is the fork decision-05 exists to prevent. Two divergences from shadcn are deliberate: the card title ships as a real heading, since a card grid of divs leaves the document with no outline, and it names the <article> it heads, a boundary a screen reader announces on entry, rather than leaving that boundary unnamed or promising the landmark role=\"region\" would; and role=\"alert\", which shadcn hard-codes on every variant, is treated as the assertive live region it is — it interrupts, so it is correct only for content that appears after load and is urgent. #Alert therefore takes a live setting defaulting to none, and the gallery ships one specimen of each of the three answers. The corollary, written beside them because it is the one that bites: a POLITE region must be mounted before its content changes, or the only change it reports is its own arrival and nothing is spoken; role=\"alert\" is the exception, announced when the element is inserted with its text already in it. The terminal injects a screen whole on navigation, so the assertive specimen speaks on every arrival at /primitives — printed on the screen, because a gallery that demonstrates a role owes the reader its cost"
	"decision-16": "The combobox ships the filtering the platform owns and nothing past it: <input list> plus <datalist>, which the UA maps to role=\"combobox\" with autocomplete=\"list\" and haspopup=\"listbox\", and a form holding no button, which submits on change — so a picked suggestion writes the row with zero script. The UA runs the popup's lifecycle and exposes no expanded state for it, so APG's required aria-expanded is one more thing neither the page nor a reader can see, printed on the screen beside the pair's other costs. The option list is stated once and emitted three times, as the datalist, the input's pattern and the column's CEL, so a control-side gate and a store-side constraint that could drift are one declaration; the pattern is load-bearing rather than decoration, because change also fires on blur after partial typing. This screen's write is a form's rather than a machine's, because a pick is a commit on a gesture (decision-17)"
	"decision-17": "One wall stands between that control and shadcn's cmdk combobox, and the screen names it rather than faking past it. Virtual focus: moving the active option is a read of the row after this one in a foreign, live-filtered collection, which row closure forbids, and neither the machine vocabulary nor the data attributes names an ordinal — what decision-08's \"no declarative precedent\" was pointing at, and the only thing it was pointing at. The two halves that are not walls are the two /command runs: an assign reading the event's value (decision-24), and a nested region whose interpolated filter tracks the enclosing row. This screen still declines the machine, and the reason is the column rather than the vocabulary: ComboboxDemo's choice enumerates five values, a per-keystroke assign would state values it does not admit, and a chart holds no pattern to refuse them while the control holds one and fires it on submit. A pick is a COMMIT, not screen state as it changes. So the two screens are the two answers — literals the UA filters, against data a column filters — and neither supersedes the other"
	"decision-18": "The switch's two-state machine is not a switch: it is the shape of every binary control, so checkbox, toggle and collapsible are one generator whose only parameter that matters is the ARIA attribute the field spells — aria-checked, aria-pressed, aria-expanded — which is decision-03 at its conclusion, and the screen prints the five-row table that makes the claim checkable rather than asserting it. Its fifth row is the switch, and it carries the one difference: the switch's region IS its affordance and carries no id, so its arrows keep the bare click where the four generated here are narrowed to a trigger — the suite erases the field, the trigger and that narrowing, and holds all five to one chart. The generator takes next, a map from state to what the trigger's click makes true next, so the key set IS the state set and a state no value names has no incoming arrow — which is exactly what the checkbox's third state needs: aria-checked speaks mixed, so the tri-state arm has three states and the cycle is APG's, a partially checked select-all checks everything and from there the control is the two-cycle, with nothing returning to mixed, because partial is a fact about the items and a machine is closed over its own row. One field cannot hold that function, and the two things that could are a machine whose states are the item subsets — decision-11's derived-ARIA-column extension, at 2^N states — and a cross-row read row closure forbids. Neither is paid: the select-all is a component of its own, whose value is a column rather than a field (decision-47), so this generator's tri-state arm is gone and what is left here is the binary shape it always was. Every arrow is narrowed to its trigger's id including the toggle's, whose region IS the affordance as the switch's is, because the id costs one attribute and the narrowing then survives a caller who wraps the button in something clickable. The collapsible is the accordion's multiple arm with one item and is described as exactly that — same trigger, same panel hidden by a rule keyed on the trigger's own aria-expanded, same two-state machine — dropping only the item set and the group wrapper. And alone in this catalog the screen defers no keyboard at all: a checkbox, a toggle button and a disclosure are all buttons, and Space and Enter press a button, so APG's required contract for all three is complete with no tabindex and no key handling; the roving-tabstop deferral belongs to the patterns that group N affordances under one label, and none of these three does"
	"decision-19": "The radio group and the segmented control are one machine under two skins, and the gallery ships the second entry by factoring rather than copying. #OneOf is the single-select chart — one state per option, one derived ARIA column per option, one arrow per (state, other option) pair whose literal assigns write the whole derived set as one row, and no arrow from a state to itself — and #ToggleGroup, #RadioGroup and #Tabs are three markups over it, so a fourth single-select entry costs a skin and no chart. What makes a radio group a radio group is therefore entirely outside the machine, and it is three things, all markup: a legend that is on the screen and names the group through aria-labelledby, where a segmented control hides its name in an aria-label; a description per option, carried by aria-describedby as a sibling the button points at — nested it would fold into the option's accessible name, since a button is named from its content, and its own id is what makes a click on it resolve to no arrow (decision-13's narrowing, load-bearing again); and one option per line, because a description per option is what a compact control has no room for. The group emits no name and no hidden input: it is the shape a form submits, but it is in no form and the write is the machine's one stated row, so the screen says so rather than dressing a control as a submit that cannot happen. The keyboard is exactly the segmented control's (decision-11), because it is the same chart: #OneOf takes a rove flag and the skins that are composite widgets set it, so the tabstop and the arrows arrive together or not at all — and with them focusin, so a Tab into the group leaves the column naming whatever the reader is standing on rather than something to reconcile later. The tablist takes the same flag and the same arrows, so the three composite single-select skins in this catalog rove and none of them spells a keyboard of its own. There is no aria-orientation, and now for the opposite reason — both axes move the tabstop, so there is no axis to tell a reader about. The menu's menuitemradio set is the same generator with the flag off (decision-29): a menu's arrows cross every item, not the radio subset, and half a keyboard there would be the failure this pattern avoids. The sharing is a checked fact rather than a claim: the suite canonicalizes both emitted charts by option index — guard included, since a copied generator drifts in the key as easily as in the target — and refuses them if they differ. That holds the two CHARTS to one, not the two generators — a copy that drifted would fail and a verbatim one would pass — so the factoring is what keeps there from being a second generator, and the check is what keeps one from mattering unnoticed"
	"decision-20": "Relative movement needs no vocabulary. A machine has no arithmetic and none is missing, because from each position the component draws the ABSOLUTE arrow the step lands on: from page three, Next is the arrow to page four, filed under the step button's id instead of that page's. Pagination and carousel are therefore one generator over an ordered list — #Ordinal, which writes one state per position and from each state an arrow to every other position plus the two neighbours — and the chart stays the complete inventory of where a reader can go, which is what makes it reviewable. #Ordinal is #OneOf generalized: the same one-state-per-item chart with the derived column's token parameterized and the step arrows and their two disabled columns added, so a fifth traversal belongs on one of those two rather than on a third copy of the loop. The two components wrap that one machine in their own markup, and what separates them is the ARIA contract and whether anything is projected. The page list is a <nav> of buttons because a page here is a column of a row and not a location, so shadcn's <a> would be a link to nowhere wearing a link's role. The carousel projects its slide off the root's bound data-state exactly as #Tabs projects a panel, and its dots are APG's slide picker rather than a tablist, because the slides are not tabpanels. That viewport is aria-live=\"polite\" and aria-atomic=\"false\", APG's required property for the element holding the slides: pressing a dot or a step moves focus nowhere, so the projection would otherwise swap one slide for another in silence. Two static attributes, and nothing in the chart. aria-current is decision-03 on a token attribute rather than a boolean one, so the column holds the attribute's own value — page for the page list, true for the dots — and the entity's constraint prints that vocabulary rather than a boolean someone translates. The ends are the honest part. At the first position no click@<prefix>-<key>-prev key exists at all, and the button says the same thing through disabled bound from dis_prev — which works because the binder REMOVES a bound boolean attribute when it interpolates empty (screen.js BOOL_ATTRS). The column holds disabled or the empty string, the missing arrow and the dead button are one generator's two emissions, and no aria-disabled is written beside it, because that would be a second spelling of what the chart already says and two spellings drift. The native costs are stated on the screen rather than hidden: a disabled button leaves the tab order, and the served placeholder is a non-empty string, so both steps are disabled until the row arrives — the right state for a control whose machine has not mounted. Three things shadcn ships are refused, each for a reason the chart makes. The ellipsis: a windowed page list is only a per-state projection and needs no new vocabulary, but hiding a button does not remove its arrow, so the chart would go on promising a jump the reader can no longer make. Auto-rotation: after: would express the advance exactly, WCAG 2.2.2 then demands a pause, and turning a timer off costs either a guard module this app does not ship or twice the states. Swipe: a machine narrows on an event type and an element id, while a gesture is a distance computed across several events that neither the event nor an assign carries. The keyboard is Tab in document order, native Space and Enter, and no roving tabindex — and unlike the three single-select skins that now rove (decision-19), this one owes nothing. A nav of buttons under role=\"group\" is not a composite widget: group makes no promise about arrow keys, APG has no pattern for a list of buttons, and every button being its own tab stop is the contract in full — the same reading decision-36 gives the rail's links. A roving tabstop here would take reachability away to buy discoverability nobody asked for. The screen's arrow census is measured off the emitted charts by #Inventory rather than written beside them, because a printed count the generator does not produce is a count that can be wrong"
	"decision-21": "Being in a state IS the pending timer. after is the relocated invoke, so a self-dismissing surface is a delay declared in the chart rather than a setTimeout in a handler: entering the state arms the wait, leaving it cancels the wait, and the terminal performs it — which is what puts the wait under ?tempo= and ?clock=manual with the component knowing about neither. A machine that could read a clock would stop being a function of its inputs and no session of it would replay, so the deadline is nowhere in the row and ToastDemo has no column for it. The way back to the pending state is a target the machine already draws: the trigger's arrow out of open targets open, a target re-enters a state even when it is the one already held, and that re-entry is the re-arm — nothing was added to the vocabulary to say it. And because a wait is armed by entry, a state declaring none arms none: closed carries no after, which is what leaves the screen at rest between clicks. That is a property of the chart, visible as a fifth arrow the moment it is lost, and it is the condition for a screen the visual battery can measure at all — a machine cycling on its own never settles"
	"decision-22": "The toast is announced by its viewport and not by itself. role=\"status\" sits on the container that is always mounted, because decision-15's corollary bites hardest here: a polite region must be mounted before its content changes, or the only change it reports is its own arrival — and a live region that appears together with its message is exactly that failure. Polite rather than assertive, and the timer is the argument: a message worth interrupting for is one no reader can be asked to catch inside three seconds, so auto-dismissal and role=\"alert\" exclude each other by construction. Nothing beside the role is written, since role=\"status\" already carries aria-live=\"polite\" and aria-atomic=\"true\"; and focus never moves to the surface, which is why the dismiss button is an ordinary tab stop rather than the only way out. The surface takes no top layer — the one place this gallery declines a primitive decision-14 would have taken — because a popover's openness is the UA's, moved by a button press, while after states a row and commands nothing: the timer would expire with the row saying closed and the surface still open, openness living in two places. What does not ship is named on the screen rather than left to be found, that refusal included — the screen is where a reader meets the z-index it costs. A queue is N rows and N regions (decision-12's multiple arm), whose missing part is the ordering and not the machine. Swipe needs pointer coordinates no event carries. Esc is decision-17's first wall one notch closer: keydown bubbles and an arrow could be drawn on it, but the event carries no key, so that arrow would fire on every key alike. A countdown bar would be a second, unsynchronised declaration of the delay that ?tempo= would pull apart. And WCAG 2.2.1's adjustable time limit is unmet: the delay is the component's parameter, authorable and not reader-adjustable, and the seam that would close it — an after key naming a Jessie module that returns milliseconds — is one this app declines, because zero JavaScript is its own promise. And the wait does not pause while the toast has focus, so the dismiss button can be hidden out from under a reader standing on it and focus falls back to the document: a held state declaring no after, entered on focusin and left on focusout, is drawable, since both bubble to the region — what the chart could not say is the resume, because leaving would arm a whole delay again rather than the remainder of the one it paused"
	"decision-23": "A value has no states, so it cannot be the machine's field: initial names one of states' keys and that set is finite and closed, which puts a continuous position in a SECOND column the same transition assigns — the terminal's own event leaf, {\"type\": \"event\", \"params\": {\"field\": \"valueAsNumber\"}}, whose field comes from a four-member allowlist and which DECLINES rather than writing NaN when the control that fired carries no such field. So every arrow reading it is narrowed to its control's id (decision-13), or it would be offered to controls that have nothing to say. What is left of the chart once the value has moved out of the field is one state and one targetless transition, and the judgement this screen exists to make is that such a chart is not a machine. A statechart's content is its branching — the claim that from HERE this event goes THERE and from elsewhere it does not — and a chart with one state makes no claim of that kind. What remains is three facts that were never the chart's: an event key narrowed to one id, a column the transition writes, and the leaf that reads the event. Together they are a BINDING, the terminal's write path with an allowlisted read attached, while field, initial and states are the ceremony the vocabulary charges for reaching it — and the entity prints that admission rather than hiding it, since phase's cel is a one-member enum and a column whose whole value set is one member carries no information. The honest spelling for a value-carrying control therefore names the column, the event and the field it reads, and invents no state to be in; this app declines to ship it, because a second write vocabulary standing beside the machine, for the case that is one arrow long, buys less than it costs, and the one-state chart is the degenerate case of the general thing. The first branch a slider grows — an arm that is disabled, dragging distinguished from committed, a threshold past which the number means something else — brings the states back doing real work with the column, the arrow and the leaf all unrespelled. The constant column is the price of ONE write path rather than two, and that trade is what this decision takes rather than a defect it reports. #Progress is the same boundary from the other side and holds nothing at all: no machine, no affordance, one bound column painted as a bar. Pointing its region at the slider's row is what makes one row drive two components — a value the reader sets and a value the reader reads — and pointing a second instance at a row nothing on the screen writes is not a broken specimen but the shape of every real progress bar, whose value belongs to whatever is making the progress. Not one aria-* attribute is written on the screen, because the platform has an element for each half: <input type=\"range\"> is a slider to every AT and <progress> is a progressbar, each supplying its role and its value off its own attributes, and both are labelable, so a <label for> a reader can see is the accessible name. Both are dressed rather than rebuilt — one accent-color paints the thumb, the rail's fill and both bars, every one of them a shadow tree no selector on the page can reach — so the exact shape of a thumb and a bar is the engine's and differs a little between them, which is the price of the sentence before it and the trade shadcn declines when it rebuilds both as div towers over Radix. The roving-tabstop deferral the rest of the catalog carries does not reach this screen, because it belongs to patterns grouping N affordances under one label and a single-thumb range is one affordance; two thumbs would be two, and that case takes what the single-select skins now take: data-rove over the column saying which thumb is current (plugins/pronto/docs/2026-09-03-the-reader-is-also-a-writer.md). Four things shadcn ships are refused, with the reason each, and one thing the binding itself cannot do is named beside them. Two thumbs, because the custom rail means rebuilding the native control's whole contract by hand, and two native ranges instead need the low thumb held under the high one, a comparison between two columns that an assign cannot carry — a guard would decide it, and decision-02's cliff is exactly there. Any arithmetic on the value, the same wall stated generally, since the column holds the number the control produced and no clamp, no snap to an uneven scale, no taper and no percentage of anything the row does not carry. Committing on release, which is one key in the chart — change for input — and is declined because every intermediate write is what there is to watch. And a row that can move the control: the binding is the value CONTENT attribute, and an <input type=\"range\"> is in value mode, so the reader's first interaction sets the dirty value flag and the thumb stops tracking what the binder writes. A row paints a bar forever and moves a slider only until it is first touched. Nothing on this screen turns on it — the control is the only writer of its row — but a consumer that adds a second writer cannot roll a refused write back where the reader can see it, which is the guarantee data-value gives a checkbox and this does not have. And an indeterminate bar, because a <progress> is indeterminate when it carries no value attribute at all, while a bound attribute is present-or-empty rather than present-or-absent — the binder removes only boolean and URL attributes when a binding comes out empty, and an empty one paints a bar at zero that no reader can tell from a real zero — so indeterminacy is not a value a column can state, but different markup"
	"decision-24": "Typed text is a column. {\"type\": \"event\", \"params\": {\"field\": \"value\"}} is the one leaf a machine states instead of importing, and it reads the EVENT rather than another row — so a transition stays decidable from the row and the event that fired it, and the chart stays the whole inventory of what can happen, which is the property a leaf reaching into the DOM would have cost. The field it may name is a closed list — value, checked, valueAsNumber, key — because a leaf naming any property would be a handle on the element; and a control carrying no such field declines the transition rather than writing false or NaN and calling it the reader's answer. The arrow is TARGETLESS, so the machine acts and stays and one state records a continuously changing value — which is why CommandDemo has exactly one state and no second thing to be in. The other half is the nested region, whose data-filter interpolates the enclosing item's column and is re-resolved when that column moves: the filtered list is regions the store answers, not markup a script rewrites, and the two together are the whole behaviour — no debounce, no list held outside the store, no app JavaScript. The shape that falls out is TWO collections. An interpolated filter is re-resolved on the parent's refresh while the region's own subscription is woken by the same write, so a region reading the very table the keystroke wrote answers from the query before last. The collection a keystroke moves and the collection a keystroke reads are therefore kept apart, and each write wakes exactly one of them. The other half is the nested region, whose data-filter interpolates the enclosing item's column and is re-resolved when that column moves — so a filtered list is regions the store answers rather than markup a script rewrites. What the option itself is belongs to the screen that carries one (decision-25)"
	"decision-25": "The palette is ROWS, and the widget is three columns of one other row. command_item holds one row per command, written by the terminal from the entity's seed: when it first opens the collection (decision-35), so the list orders itself, a seventh command is a row rather than a schema change, and what the reader sees is what the store answered rather than fixed markup with the misses hidden. The three columns are not interchangeable. query is what was typed; active is the option aria-activedescendant points at; chosen is the option that was run. One column doing two of those jobs is a palette that runs a command every time a reader arrows past it — the same split the calendar makes between its caret and its date. The machine's one arrow writes query from the event leaf and clears active with a literal in the same breath, because an option the filter has dropped is not rendered and the form an arrow would name is not there (decision-35). Choosing is a form, and so is moving. Each option owns a pick form stating both columns and a move form stating the neighbour the projection named; the arrow keys are data-key naming the caret's own form, and before the first arrow {active} is empty, so the pair the keys reach is the one a hidden limit=1 region stamps from the head of the current read. Enter is not a data-key — the binding admits the arrows, Home and End, and nothing else — and does not need to be: the field sits in a form carrying chosen bound to {active}, which Enter submits natively. Without that the caret would move and never choose, which is worse than shipping no keyboard. What the shape costs is the two labelled groups shadcn draws. A region renders one read, so two groups are two reads, and the projection answers next within a read — the arrows would stop at the boundary. The group rides each option as its own text. Buying the headings back means a third region over all the rows emitting the move forms in the global order while the visible groups render, which is a shape this screen has not taken rather than one it cannot. Still absent, and for reasons the rows did not touch: an aria-expanded that can be false, because binding it needs a column on the row holding the QUERY that knows what the option read returned, and the projection answers on the rows a region holds — so the attribute is a literal and is honest only while the read answers something; a count, for the same wall from the other side, since the projection's count answers on each option row and not on the row holding the query; Home and End, because every clause in the closed set names a NEIGHBOUR and an extremum is neither; and Ctrl+K, whose reason has changed rather than held: a guard is a module and a module tests any key it likes, so a chord is expressible — the grid answers one. What this palette wants is a chord pressed from ANYWHERE, and every listener the terminal attaches is a region\\'s, so where an event comes from has one answer and a shortcut outside the surface has no source to come from"
	"decision-26": "The platform's answer for a select predates every machine in this gallery and needs no component tier at all: an <optgroup> carrying a data-live IS a region, so the prompt option and the authored group survive its sweep while the option rows fill the group below them, and the select's own name feeds the form it sits in — no hidden field, no widget, and a form holding no <button> submits on change, so a pick commits with zero script. The picker and this select are two honest answers, and the question that decides between them is whether the options are data: a machine's states are literals, so no chart can be written over an option set that does not exist at build time, which is why SelectDemo's column states a length where PickerDemo's enumerates. What the native control buys is everything the UA owns — the whole keyboard contract, typeahead, and the operating system's own presentation on a phone, none of it written here and none of it deferred, so the roving-tabstop deferral (plugins/pronto/docs/2026-09-01-aria-is-columns.md) that the grouped patterns carry does not apply to it at all. What it spends is the binding: a form resets on success, so after a pick the control shows its prompt again and the row is where the choice lives. The picker is a bound state; this is a committed act, which is how the precedent in apps/xpense uses it, and neither reading supersedes the other. Bound selection AND live options cannot be had together today, and the reason is mechanical rather than a matter of taste: binding the control back means a machine (the event leaf writes the value the control was showing straight into the row), a machine region is a slot, a region nested inside a slot is never hydrated, and the live option group has to sit inside the select — while the list region that would escape that draws nothing until its first row exists, and nothing seeds a tab-tier collection. That last fact is also why the option rows are the reader's: the minting form ships with the control, because the grammar an option id takes is one sentence emitted twice, as the input's pattern and as the column's cel. And it is why removing an option does not ship — a pick is text and not a reference, and this tier arbitrates nothing between two collections, so a delete would leave a pick naming a row that is gone. Corrected by decision-35. “Nothing seeds a tab-tier collection” is false: the terminal writes an entity's seed: rows when it first opens the collection. So the option rows need not be the reader's — a seeded list draws from the first frame — and the minting form here is a demonstration of the grammar rather than the only way an option can exist. The rest of the wall stands exactly as written: binding the control back still means a machine, a machine region is still a slot, and a region nested in a slot is still never hydrated. Seeding removes the second reason this note gives, not the first. The projection is a third route this note could not name and this app has not tried: an <optgroup> region may answer {\"eq\": [\"id\", \"{choice}\"]} against the enclosing row and pick between two option templates with data-when, one carrying selected and one not, which would bind the control back with no machine at all"
	"decision-27": "Breadcrumb, avatar, separator and skeleton have no state AND no variants, so neither of the two things a screen prints in its closing pane exists for them: no chart, because a machine holds a field and none of these has one, and no variant→token map either, because none of them has a variant. What is left is the accessibility tree, and for a component whose whole content is that tree the tree IS the machine — so it is printed as data and folded through the emitted markup in both directions, refusing a node a row describes that the markup does not carry, an attribute a row names that the node lacks, and any role or aria-* on the screen — or alt on an image — that no row lists. Three of the four turn on one question — is this element content, or a picture of something the markup already states — and all three answer aria-hidden rather than role=\"presentation\", which strips the element's own role and leaves its subtree in the tree: a breadcrumb's slashes would still be spoken between every crumb. The breadcrumb's current page is text carrying aria-current=\"page\" and nothing else, diverging from shadcn's role=\"link\" with aria-disabled=\"true\", which announces a control that cannot be used where the page a reader is already on is no control at all; its ancestors declare nothing, because an <a href> is already a link. The collapsed middle is refused as unearned rather than unreachable: a menu of hidden crumbs is a popover a trigger commands, exactly what decision-14 already ships, and a trail two deep has nothing to window. The trail the screen emits is the route's real one: this catalog is flat, so /breadcrumb's only ancestor is / (decision-28), and a crumb standing in for an ancestry the app does not have would be the one overclaim on a screen whose whole content is what it tells a reader. The avatar's fallback is not a swap but what is underneath — the initials are the element's own content and the image paints over them, so an image that never paints leaves them visible with no error handler anywhere. That is why the name sits on the wrapper under role=\"img\" and the image carries alt=\"\": an alt holding the name would paint the name over the initials the moment the bytes failed, and would move the accessible name depending on whether they arrived. Standing alone and standing beside the name are one definition's two answers, because a picture next to the name as text is decoration and takes aria-hidden. What the missing error event costs is stated rather than worked around: loaded, failed and loaded-transparent are indistinguishable to the page, so nothing records which happened, and Radix's delayMs — the same observation with a timer on it — has no spelling here. The separator ships both answers, because the element is one line and the tree is not: decorative where a heading or a landmark already draws the boundary, semantic where the line is the only thing drawing it. The semantic horizontal is <hr>, whose implicit role is separator and whose default orientation is horizontal, so it declares nothing at all; the vertical one has no element, so it declares both the role and the orientation. APG's window splitter is refused for the reason decision-20 refuses swipe: a drag is a distance across several events, and neither an event nor an assign carries one. The skeleton's bars are aria-hidden, because a placeholder is the shape of content that has not arrived and announcing it announces nothing. aria-busy is not written: it belongs on the element whose content is on its way, which is the caller's and not the placeholder's, and on a screen where nothing is loading it would be a lie — shadcn's Skeleton writes neither. The pulse runs for a multiple of --motion-base rather than a literal, so the design system's reduced-motion block stops it, and the shape vocabulary is closed so no instance carries a length of its own. The screen emits no DOM id at all, which is the readable consequence of having nothing that fires: decision-13's narrowing has nothing to narrow"
	"decision-28": "The strip is one row on every route and its length is the CATALOG's, so it grows with every component that graduates and a label crosses the right edge of a 400px board however short the labels are cut — shortening titles to one word bought one wave, and seventeen routes spent it. The catalog is the only place a reader starts from: every demo screen is reached from a card in its grid, which is exactly the case strip: false is for. So the strip carries the catalog alone — and a strip of one link is not a strip, because the terminal draws no nav below two routes. None is drawn here. What carries a reader back is one link in each screen's own header, whose width is the route's DEPTH rather than the catalog's size, so it cannot outgrow a board as the roster grows and no screen added later makes it worse. A trail rather than a link is refused: a breadcrumb is a navigation landmark carrying an aria-label and an aria-current on every screen it appears on, and this catalog is one level deep — ceremony where a link is the whole statement, and a screen whose claim is that not one aria-* is written on it (decision-23) would lose that claim to its own chrome. What the trade costs is named rather than hidden: /slider to /toast is two moves through the catalog where it was one, and nothing persistent says where in the roster a reader is. Against which the scrolled strip was not answering that question either — every label past the fifth was off the board, so it answered for five routes and hid the other twelve. shell/shared/chrome.css is the one stylesheet that dresses the link, as it dressed the strip — but it carries nothing the link needs. A shared sheet reaches a screen through the @import at the top of that screen's own sheet, which is a request of its own: for the round trip before it lands the link is a 16px line box. A target size a screen guarantees cannot be one that arrives late, so the 24px box is the arrow's own element and no length in the stylesheet restates it"
	"decision-29": "shadcn's DropdownMenu and its Menubar are one surface under two containers: a single generator emits the popover carrying role=\"menu\", and the two definitions differ only in whether one trigger stands in front of it or a role=\"menubar\" of them. The bar holds triggers and nothing else — a menubar admits the children a menu admits and a menu is not one of them, so every surface stands outside the bar and is placed by anchor-name against its trigger's id. Where a top-layer element sits in the document costs nothing, which is what makes that legal rather than merely tidy. A menu's accessibility tree admits menuitem, menuitemradio, menuitemcheckbox, group and separator, and nothing else. So a group heading a reader can see is not a node the tree may hold: it carries the id the group's aria-labelledby names and is aria-hidden, which is how one string names the group and shows above it without being written twice — a hidden node still counts when aria-labelledby points straight at it. That is decision-19's legend inverted, where the name is a node outside the group and stays in the tree, and it is the same rule that keeps the tick inside a checked item aria-hidden instead of letting a glyph join the item's accessible name. A plain item is an <a href>. A menu item invokes a command; behavior here is data and a machine writes rows (decision-02), so a navigation is the one command this app performs with no handler module, and every plain item's href is a route the app serves — checked against the emitted routes rather than asserted. A stateful item is a REGION OF ITS OWN, because a machine binds one row and a region nested inside a singleton region is never hydrated: the surface holds no data-live, each menuitemcheckbox is the switch's binary chart narrowed to its own button id, and each menuitemradio set is the single-select chart on the group element. This screen adds no chart to the catalog; it adds two roles and a container. No shortcut hint stands beside any item. A chord is expressible — the grid answers Ctrl+End (its acceptance) — and what a menu item wants is one pressed from anywhere, which has no source: every listener is a region\\'s. A hint printed beside an item whose chart draws no such arrow would be an overclaim living in the markup either way. The dropdown surface keeps the promise role=\"menu\" makes. Its items are one row and its region is the surface, which is the whole of what let a caret cross them: an item that owned a region of its own could never be walked by one, because a region's members are its own. Up and Down walk the six items, Home and End name the ends, focusin records a reader who tabbed, and typing jumps — an arrow per destination it can be selected from, guarded on the letters so far, with the buffer a column of the same row and a targetless timer clearing it a second after the last keystroke. Per destination it can be SELECTED from, because a destination whose disambiguating prefix is two keystrokes long is reached only from the state the first of them lands in: the same arrow drawn elsewhere is one no reader could ever select, and the reader gets there anyway by walking two arrows the chart does draw. No tabindex is emitted: every item stays in the Tab sequence and the caret is data-focus, the accordion's reading (decision-12) rather than the tablist's. The menubar's three surfaces and the context menu still take the older sentence — click and native Space/Enter, no arrows, no tabindex — and the bar's own tab stop with them. What changed is not the argument but what it costs: the caret is a chart the surface runs beside its items', so the remaining surfaces are a row each and no new vocabulary. The dropdown shipped first because it is the one with items of three kinds, which is what a caret crossing regions could not have done. The earlier reading of this deferral ships anyway, because this catalog's line is drawn at whether the pattern is REACHABLE without the deferred keys — which is why the palette claims neither role=\"combobox\" nor role=\"listbox\", an option there being unreachable at all without aria-activedescendant, and why a menu falls on the other side: every item keeps its own tab stop, every item is operable, the deferral takes the shortcut and not the capability, and the half APG guards hardest — Esc, light dismiss and focus returning to the invoker — is the browser's and arrives whole. A roving tabstop with no arrows to move it would strand every item but one"
	"decision-30": "The context menu ships, and what it cost the terminal is two things neither the machine nor CSS could supply. Suppressing the browser's own menu is preventDefault on the contextmenu event. Narrowing was never the problem — any bubbling type is an event key, so contextmenu@<id> selects a transition exactly as a click does — but a transition's whole vocabulary is a target, assigns and a raise, and none of the three cancels anything, so a right-click fired the arrow AND opened the UA's menu. The claim that ruled it out did not move: every cancel the terminal makes belongs to a gesture the markup DECLARED — a form's submit, a drag's dragover and drop, a data-key binding's keydown. What changed is which markup counts. An arrow keyed on contextmenu IS such a declaration, as much as a data-key map is, so contextmenu joins a closed set of DISPLACING types whose default is cancelled wherever an arrow answers them. The set is closed and this is the whole of it, so an arrow can only ever displace a default that was incoherent to keep: an app menu and the UA's cannot both be what the reader asked for. And the cancel is the ARROW's rather than the type's — narrowed to the affordance the chart names, so a right-click elsewhere in the same region keeps the platform's own menu, and one in a state that draws no such arrow keeps it too. A whole region made inert by one narrowed arrow inside it would be the fix costing more than the bug. Openness is the second. commandfor acts on activation and a right-click is not one, so no invoker can open this surface; data-open lets a column decide a surface's place in the top layer, re-derived on every bind and idempotent, so a replay puts it exactly where the session had it. Only opening needed the terminal: the way back is the auto popover's own light dismiss and Escape, and the chart draws no arrow home rather than spelling a dismissal the element already performs. Placement is the row that did NOT need a fix, and the reason is worth keeping. anchor-name still places a surface against an ELEMENT and never against a point — that was true and remains true. What the row holds is where inside the target the pointer was, as a share of its own box in parts per thousand (ContextMenuDemo), and the stylesheet multiplies it back out against anchor-size(). Framing the point on the element rather than the viewport is what makes it storable at all: viewport pixels would need a window size pinned beside them for a replay to mean anything, and a window resized mid-session makes any one pin a lie. So no length is stored, the pixels are recomputed rather than remembered, and no chart can reach the geometry the share was measured against — idempotence under a resize is structural rather than a contract anyone keeps. The target takes a tabindex, alone on the screen: a keyboard reader raises this menu with Shift+F10 or the Menu key, both of which fire contextmenu on the FOCUSED element, so an unfocusable panel would carry a menu only a mouse could open"
	"decision-31": "Sheet, drawer and alert dialog are three names for decision-14's #Dialog, and the served markup says so. The sheet is that dialog given an edge, which reaches the DOM as one data-edge attribute the screen's stylesheet pins with a margin and carries with a translate — no second element, no variant class, no script. The drawer is the sheet with that edge fixed to bottom, a name rather than a definition: what makes vaul's drawer its own component upstream is drag-to-dismiss, and a drag is pointer events, which is script — so the grab handle is not drawn either. Both parameters unify modal to true rather than offering the choice, because a panel pinned to the side of a page that is still live is one a reader Tabs out of without closing, and a question asked over one is not being asked; the incoherent combinations are therefore unspellable. The alert dialog is the one that differs by CONTRACT and not by skin, and the screen prints that contract clause by clause because the element keeps most of it. Radix's AlertDialog differs from its own Dialog in four places: role=\"alertdialog\", a description it insists on, focus defaulted to the cancel control, and a press outside that does not dismiss. The fourth arrives with the element — a modal <dialog> never had outside dismissal to give up — so the row that reads as a lack on /overlays is the row that reads as the contract here, and the other three are markup: the role, the description that is required rather than advisable and that #Dialog required from its first line, and autofocus on the least destructive control. The finding is the one clause nobody keeps: a close request cannot be refused declaratively. closedby=\"none\" is the attribute and is outside this tree's measured engine matrix (2026-08-03-component-tier.md); a cancel listener is the other route and is script. So Escape ends the question here as it ends Radix's, and what ships is the deliberate answer rather than an undismissable surface. The outcome still reaches no row, which on this screen is a choice and not a wall — an arrow narrowed to the answer's id would record which control ended it — and it is the only absence here that a rung rather than the platform is holding"
	"decision-32": "The shell owns validation and the browser does not: screen.js sets form.noValidate on every form it wires, so the UA's bubble — a surface no stylesheet reaches, no storyboard frame holds and no visual battery photographs — is replaced by a screen STATE beside a paragraph the page owns, and checkValidity() is the whole gate, a form failing it stating no row, reaching no store and leaving the screen in validation-error until the next keystroke, which is the only thing that clears one. What the shell reveals is the FIRST .invalid in the submitting form and nothing else, so one message per form is the entire vocabulary and a second gated field would be refused in the first one's words — which is why exactly one control here carries a pattern, generated from the same sentence as its column's cel, and the other carries a maxlength, a limit the control keeps by truncating rather than by refusing, leaving nothing a submit could be turned down for. Both controls bind their DEFAULT value rather than their shown one, the input through its value ATTRIBUTE and the textarea through its own text, which is what a textarea's default value IS, so the reset the shell performs on an accepted write lands on the row where data-value — which writes the shown value and leaves the default where it was — would land on blank. aria-invalid is written nowhere and is not reachable, the verdict being checkValidity()'s and taken inside the shell's submit listener: it is no column, so no binding carries it, and the invalid event that names each refused control does not bubble, so no region's delegated listener sees it either — what reaches the accessibility tree is the message itself under role=\"alert\", announced on the reveal with its text already in it, decision-15's one exception used for the one case it is for, and named by NO aria-describedby, since accname does not skip a hidden node a description references and the reference would read the refusal into the control's description before anything was typed, which is decision-16's finding applied rather than repeated. .store-error is the other refusal the shell reveals and this screen cannot reach it, every collection in the gallery being tab-tier and therefore an in-memory table with no constraint to break, so it is named rather than shipped, a paragraph nothing can reveal being a claim nothing checks. The delete sits inside a LIST region over the one pinned row rather than beside the fields, so the affordance and the row it acts on arrive together and a delete of a row nothing wrote is unreachable rather than refused — which is also what makes empty the state the screen opens in, a top-level list region with no rows being what writes it. And two button variants ship rather than shadcn's six, because a variant rendered with nothing behind it is a control that does nothing, which decision-09's catalog rule refuses, and there are two pieces of work on the screen — a reset button, whose HTML algorithm is real work with no listener behind it, being refused outright by the interpreter's control lint as reaching nothing, so the only reset here is the shell's own and the default-value binding is what it is for. The closing pane prints the form OBJECT where every other screen prints its chart, the same declaration the program's surface carries and the controls were emitted from, so a field that drifted out of the markup would drift out of the pane with it"
	"decision-33": "A table is the first component here whose content is a COLLECTION, and everything follows from refusing to make it anything else. /command answers the same tab tier with rows too (decision-25), so the rows stay rows and the minting form ships with the component the way /select's does — a demonstration rather than a necessity now the tier seeds (decision-35), with the columns stated once and emitted three times, as the header row, the row template, and that form's controls, so a column reaching one of the three and not the others cannot exist. The semantics are the elements' own: a <caption> stating the order, th scope=\"col\" in the head, th scope=\"row\" on each row's first cell, and not one aria-* attribute, because a table that says role=\"table\" repeats its own tag name. Filtering SHIPS, at an arity /command never reached — its filtered regions each bind ONE row, while here a nested LIST region's filter interpolates the enclosing row's query column and reads again when it moves. The spelling that makes that work is load-bearing rather than stylistic: a region's render sweeps every child that is not one of its rows, the <template data-item> included, so a nested region that re-hydrates finds no template, reads as a slot, and throws on the second matching row — the row template is therefore NAMED, and the <tbody> points at it with data-template, a named template living in the screen's own map, which no render sweeps. Sorting does NOT ship, and its price is the reason rather than its possibility. A region's order is data-order and the interpreter reads it RAW, so no column interpolates into it and no machine writes it — but a region's item templates may be narrowed by data-when matched against the row, and an arm carrying a <tbody> whose data-order is that arm's LITERAL needs no interpolation at all. The chart is not what is missing either: the columns are known, so four of them in two directions are eight states, which is the single-select chart once more. What a sort costs here is a stamped copy of the table per order and the affordance moved off the column headers — a header sits in the frame region and a machine hears only its own — and it is refused for that. One more item lands on that bill once arrow keys ship, since every arm then carries the step forms as well as the table. What is missing is the wire from the column such a chart would write to the read the region performs, and that wire is one attribute's interpolation plus a re-hydration that noticed an order that moved where today it compares only the filter — the honest contrast with /pagination, where movement is expressible because the component draws an absolute arrow per position and needs no wire at all. The chart this screen does carry states the query row on arrival: after: {\"0\": \"typing\"} aimed at the row the table's frame hangs off, because a table has to be on the screen before anyone types where /command's palette may wait for a keystroke — so empty is the one beat every arrival meets rather than a resting state, and the state the arrow lands in declares no wait — an arrow that exists only because the query row cannot be stated any other way, which a seeded cursor row deletes together with the pending state, and why /data-table opens in populated with no empty beat. The two CSS-only components carry one fact each that is not CSS. A scroll box is a tab stop, so it takes tabindex=\"0\" and a name, or a keyboard reader cannot move its content and lands in a region that announces nothing — the /select review's finding folded into the component, with the axis as its only parameter, because how far a box scrolls is the stylesheet's business; and an aspect ratio is one property under a closed vocabulary of classes, #Skeleton's rule, so no instance carries a length of its own. Four things are refused with the reason each: an aggregate row, because a sum is a read across rows and a region hands its template one row at a time; the header's tri-state box, an aggregate over the rows under it that has to land on the row owning the list while every clause answers on the rows the region itself holds — row selection is neither refused nor a machine to price, a chosen row being a derived column, {\"eq\": [\"id\", \"{cursor}\"]} answered on the region, which is what /data-table ships; deleting a row, which would work and is left out because a per-row control that only removes turns a component demo into a CRUD screen; and APG's grid keyboard, a per-cell tabindex the terminal never writes, its row-level half closed on /data-table by data-key over the projection's next and prev, deferred with the same sentence the other screens use and with no partial roving tabindex in its place"
	"decision-34": "A chart names a Jessie leaf where it cannot compute the value it needs, and nowhere else. The rule the brief opened with — no app JavaScript at all — was a claim about behaviour that the catalog kept for twenty-one screens, and the calendar is where it stops being expressible: a month is arithmetic over a date, and a machine has no arithmetic and no clock. What survives unqualified is the part that mattered: no screen carries a data-on-* binding or a <script>, so no DOM event is ever handed to app code, and a module reachable any other way is refused. A leaf is called with the reduce's signature over the row alone, in a compartment endowed with nothing, so it can read no clock, reach no DOM and see no other row — which is why a computed value does not widen what a chart is decidable from: the arrows are still all there, and only one of them carries a number the chart did not write itself. The test moved with the claim rather than being relaxed: every module a route lists must be named by a chart on that screen, so a module that arrived as a DOM handler fails even though the old assertion — a route lists nothing — would now be the thing that was wrong"
	"decision-35": "A table's state belongs to its rows, and the projection is what lets it live there. One attribute on the <tbody> answers three derived columns out of a closed set — chosen as {\"eq\": [\"id\", \"{cursor}\"]} and nxt/prv as the neighbour on either side — and the answers merge into each row before binding, so aria-selected=\"{chosen}\" is an ordinary attribute binding and nothing downstream learns a projection was there. That is one column on one row where every compile-time item set in this catalog spends an invented prefix per option — sel_, chk_, exp_, inc_, cur_ — plus an arrow per ordered pair writing N literal assigns on each. And it is what makes the item set DATA: a ninth invoice is a row, where a ninth tab is a recompiled machine. The derived columns never reach a transition, because the region keeps the stored rows for a machine to read — so a chart stays decidable from the row and the event that fired it, which is the property a comparison leaking into a guard would have cost. The keyboard is those same columns plus one attribute. data-key maps ArrowDown and ArrowUp to a form id interpolated from the cursor row and read at event time, so a key submits the form the chosen row owns and DOM focus never moves: no chart, no arithmetic, no focus() and no module, which is the composition APG's virtual-focus model was already asking for. Home and End do not ship, because no clause names an END of a set — next and prev name a neighbour, and the first and last rows are not neighbours of anything. Cell-level arrows do not ship, because every clause answers about a ROW and a cell is not one; the cells here are static text, so the row-level half is the whole of what a reader can move between. The native role is overridden to grid, and that is a promise rather than a label. decision-33 keeps the table's semantics the elements' own and writes not one aria-*; a widget with a tab stop of its own and a selection is a different thing, and role=\"grid\" is what tells a reader the arrows belong to it — which, as decision-29 put it, makes the role itself a promise about the keyboard. What it promises and this screen does not keep is cell-level movement and Home/End, so the promise is scoped in the prose beside it rather than left for a reader to find by pressing a key that does nothing. The five aria-* attributes that come with the grid are the widget's and not the table's: aria-labelledby and aria-activedescendant on the grid, aria-selected on a row. The empty cursor is a real spelling rather than a hole. The arrow that records a keystroke also clears the choice, because a row the filter has removed is not rendered: neither the id aria-activedescendant points at nor the forms the keys submit exist. The ids that clearing resolves to are owned by a limit=1 list over the same read — a LIST, because a slot's filter has to witness one row and a cap is not a witness — which enters at the first row of what is left rather than earning a fourth clause. A read that matches nothing renders no pair at all, and there the stylesheet is load-bearing: with .dt-body :empty the grid is not drawn, so it is not a tab stop and no arrow can reach for a form the read never rendered. The rows are SEEDED, which is this screen's other half and the correction it carries to decision-25, decision-26 and decision-33. A tab entity has no migration to render seed: rows into, so the terminal writes them itself when it first opens the collection; a read prepares its collection before it answers, so the seeded rows are there for the first read and this screen has no empty beat and no arrival arrow. The cursor row is seeded from the machine's own initial world — one CUE value emitted as the seed, as the machine's context and as the readout's data-empty-row — so decision-07's agreement holds by construction rather than by review. No positional ARIA attribute ships on a row, and both pairs are refused with the reason rather than deferred. aria-rowcount and aria-rowindex count the TABLE's rows, its header row included, and the projection answers about the region's own — so the index is off by the header, and the count has nowhere to land, since aria-rowcount belongs on the grid element while every answer arrives on a row; ARIA asks for neither while every row is in the DOM, which is the case here. aria-posinset and aria-setsize are the pair the projection's index and count clauses could answer, and ARIA 1.2 forbids them on a row that descends from a table or a grid — with aria-level and aria-expanded they are a treegrid's hierarchy, which this is not. So a row here carries exactly one ARIA state, aria-selected, and the projection is three clauses rather than five"
	"decision-36": "The two navigation components are assemblies of parts already shipped, and the screen's job is to prove that rather than to add a third menu. The bar is decision-29's menubar with a link for every item; the rail's collapse is decision-03's binary chart under aria-expanded; the narrow board is decision-31's left-edge sheet. So the whole screen carries one machine, and it is the generator five other controls already run — the checkbox, the toggle, the collapsible, the menu's checkbox item, and the accordion's multiple arm one per row. What is new is the rail's CONTENT. A tab entity may now declare seed: — it has no migration to render the rows into, so the terminal writes them when it first opens the collection — which retires the constraint decision-25, decision-26 and decision-33 each argued from, and makes this the first item set in the app that is data rather than a CUE-time list a machine's states were built from. Nothing freezes it, because a rail needs no machine: a sixth page is a row. aria-current is therefore derived rather than authored. The rail is two regions deep because the comparison needs both sides — the outer binds the row naming the open route, the inner projects {\"current\": {\"eq\": [\"id\", \"{current}\"]}} over the items — and a binding interpolates without evaluating, so two rows have nowhere in the markup to meet. What the attribute binds is the projection's own answer, \"true\" or \"false\", both in aria-current's vocabulary. The pattern's token \"page\" would come from an item template narrowed by data-when=\"current=eq.true\", which the interpreter runs and the emitter refuses: its kind lint checks a data-when's columns against the entity's declared fields alone and knows nothing of a region's data-project widening that set. The weaker value ships, and the gap is named on the screen rather than worked around with a second stored column. Which route is open is a fact the PROGRAM states and not one the terminal supplies: a screen is handed only the params its path declares, and a static route declares none. That is the honest limit of this screen's aria-current rather than a wire someone forgot. The roles are the divergence worth naming, and it is open rather than settled. shadcn's NavigationMenu is a plain <nav> with a list; APG's menu pattern is written for a list of CHOICES — actions and functions — though it also carries a navigation menubar example. What this catalog has is the menu surface, whose one handler-free command is a navigation (decision-29), so the bar wears the roles that surface already carries and the landmark around it is what tells a reader the region is navigation. The cost of reading it the other way is the one decision-29 already priced: role=\"menubar\" promises a reader that the arrows belong to the bar. The bar itself carries no aria-label: a widget inside a landmark of the same name hands a reader the string twice. Three things do not ship, each with its own reason. The hover-opened panel, because interestfor is implemented in no engine — the wall decision-14 already prints. The shared viewport that resizes to whichever panel is open, because its box has to be measured from the panel inside it and a measurement is script. And shadcn's collapsible=\"icon\" rail, because an icon per item is a column these rows do not carry and a glyph per route in CSS would be exactly the compile-time list the rows were adopted to retire — so what ships is its offcanvas default. The keyboard splits cleanly, and the screen says so rather than filing everything under one sentence. The rail asks for nothing at all: APG has no composite pattern for a list of links, so every link being its own tab stop is the contract in full, and no roving tabindex, no aria-activedescendant and no key binding is emitted anywhere. The two tabindex attributes that ARE emitted are the prose tables' — a box that scrolls is a tab stop that has to be named, which is decision-33's rule and not this screen's own. The bar is missing APG's roving tabstop, and that gap is now open for a NARROWER reason than it was — next/prev projected onto each row and data-key submitting a named form on an arrow key together close the keyboard for the patterns APG gives virtual focus, listbox, combobox, grid and tree, where moving is moving a selection and DOM focus never leaves the container. A menubar is not one of them: it moves real focus between its triggers, which is a DOM write with a reader on the other end of it and a mechanism nobody has built"
	"decision-37": "Popover ships as a component of its own because four definitions in this catalog are already it with something added, and each of them re-derived it: the tip is this surface with text in it, the non-modal dialog is it plus a heading, a description, role=\"dialog\" naming both and a dismiss, the picker's listbox is it carrying role=\"listbox\" over a region, and the menu surface is it carrying role=\"menu\" over a tree that admits items alone. So #Popover is the generalisation those four should have been built on, the screen states that relationship as data, and NONE of the four is refactored here: each is its screen's reviewed contract, and folding four artifacts onto a fifth is a change to four screens for a factoring this one only has to state. What closes the deferral is named rather than left open: one of the four is the terminal's own omni.#Picker, so a fifth spelling this app owns cannot absorb it, and the fold lands when the pairing graduates into terminal.cue — at which point the other three follow a definition they can all reach, and not before. What the definition holds is the pairing of one trigger with one surface and the area that surface asks for, and nothing else — which is why there is no entity, no machine and no module, and why the closing pane prints the placement where a stateful screen prints its chart. place is a position-area keyword rather than a side of its own, so the attribute the skin keys on and the property it sets are one spelling, and each fallback is that keyword's mirror on the axis it places on, DERIVED from the place's own name rather than written beside it, because an axis written twice is an axis that can disagree with itself. Placement is the one clause on the screen the element does not keep — an unplaced popover opens in the middle of the viewport — so it is three CSS properties, and the fallback is demonstrated by an anchor flush with the column's edge whose requested area cannot hold its surface, the browser deciding at paint with nothing measured. popover=\"manual\" is rendered rather than described because a gift is only measurable against its absence: withdrawing light dismiss and Escape leaves the surface's own button as the only way out, so dismiss is required there and a manual popover with none cannot be spelled. What is refused is named with the reason each — a hover-opened surface, interestfor being in no engine (decision-14); a surface placed at a point rather than against an element, anchor-name having no spelling for one (decision-30); and any keyboard beyond the trigger's native Space and Enter, arrow keys inside a surface belonging to its content, which here is prose"
	"decision-38": "The calendar is decision-34's single instance, and the leaf is drawn exactly at the value. A month away from a month is arithmetic; a #Machine's assign holds a literal or a leaf and has no third spelling, so the two arrows stay in the chart and the numbers they write come from one module. It is called with the reduce's signature over the calendar row alone, and the same module serves both arrows and both instances on the page through params — which is what params are for: a threshold is data in the chart, not shape in the chart. So the seeded window's ends ride as two literals and the leaf CLAMPS to them; it never decides. An arrow at either end names the month it is already on, which is precisely what the ARIA projection's next and prev answer at the ends of a list. The leaf's third emission is active and not value, which is the whole of what keeps this a date picker. active is the cell aria-activedescendant points at; value is the cell aria-selected marks and both readouts print. An arrow key and a month step write the first, a pick writes both, and one column serving both states would be a committed date that moved every time the reader paged the month — the rule plugins/pronto/docs/2026-09-01-aria-is-columns.md states as naming a column for the ARIA state it answers and nothing else. What the compartment endows is nothing (endow: () => ({})), and that settles the one thing this screen would most like to say and cannot: a day is today. A clock is not in scope for a leaf, and the terminal's own clock reaches two places — a machine's after delay and a form's {now} hidden value — neither of which is a date a chart can read, the second being a gesture besides. So aria-current=\"date\" is absent rather than pointed at a day the program picked. The module is reachable one way only, as a chart's leaf, and that is what accept-zero-js now measures in place of an empty handler list"
	"decision-39": "The day cells are ROWS, and the rows are SEEDED — the answer decision-25 and decision-33 both argued from the absence of. seed: renders into 900_seed.sql at a server tier and this app has none, so the terminal writes the rows itself when it first opens the collection: two hundred and ten cells — five grids of forty-two — and the two calendar rows they are shown on, each of which IS its instance's machine-initial world, so the row a region renders and the row a machine would synthesize are one declaration, and the screen carries neither an arrival arrow nor a data-empty-row. Seeded rather than minted because a machine writes ONE row, its own: no arrow can produce a month. That is the whole reason the leaf's arithmetic is unbounded while the months are not, and why the window is stated twice on purpose — as the seed's extent, and as the two literals the leaf clamps to — rather than as five states and eight arrows that a sixth month would have to be added to. A grid is always six weeks of seven, so every role=\"row\" is full, a cell's position in its week IS its aria-colindex, and a month step can keep the reader's square because every grid has all forty-two of them. Each cell carries the month the DAY is in beside the month it is DRAWN in, so the neighbouring months' days are the ones where the two disagree: an eq the projection answers, not a boolean the seed would have to keep true. The keyboard ships in both dimensions off that same projection — next/prev over the grid's reading order for left and right, and over a second region ordered down each weekday column for up and down, since a region declares one order and two orders are two regions. An order alone was never the lane, though: the neighbour at a column's foot was the NEXT column's head, so a caret walking down a Monday arrived on a Tuesday. The clauses take a partition now — {\"next\": \"dow\"} is the neighbour among the cells sharing this one's weekday — and an end of the partition names itself. Horizontally nothing is partitioned, deliberately: a month's days are continuous, so a week's edge is no edge and only the grid's own ends stand still. Home and End arrive with the same pair at the other end of the vocabulary, first and last, partitioned by the row's own week — so they reach the ends of the week the caret is in rather than the corners of the grid, which is what a date grid means by the two keys. That the walk's partition and the ends' differ on one lane is the point: the arrows walk the month, and Home is the start of a week. What is still absent is APG's paging at an edge — an arrow off the last row moving into the next month. Paging is the chart's move and a key that submits a form cannot reach it, so the arrow stays inside the grid and the screen says so"
	"decision-40": "A binding may target a STYLESHEET, and this is the first one in THIS catalog that does — though not the first anywhere: apps/xpense and apps/truco bind columns into a style already, which is the reason the paragraph below is a rule this screen keeps rather than one the platform enforces. The group carries style=\"--split: {split}\", the first pane is flex: 0 0 calc(var(--split) * 1%) and the second takes what is left, so one number states both widths and they cannot disagree. That is where the arithmetic a projection has none of belongs — the same trade pagination makes from the other side, drawing an absolute arrow per position rather than adding one to anything: a platform without arithmetic keeps working as long as something downstream of it can count. What makes it safe is WHO writes the column. The only writer is an <input type=\"range\">, which hands the leaf a valueAsNumber the browser has already clamped to the control's own bounds, so no reader-typed text reaches a style — where a value carrying its own semicolon would be a second declaration nobody wrote. The rule this screen ships under is therefore narrower than the binding it uses: a column a reader can type into stays out of a stylesheet until the binder validates the CHANNEL rather than the writer. Nothing in the interpreter checks that today — the channel is open, and promoting it to an idiom is what makes closing it worth doing. The handle is a control BENEATH the panes rather than the bar between them. A native range is the only pointer-continuous input this vocabulary has and its track is the whole scale, so a thumb rendered as the divider means the range overlays the group and every click in a pane jumps the split to where the pointer landed. The trade is named rather than paid: what it costs is shadcn's look, and what it keeps is APG's whole keyboard — arrows, Home, End and the page step — none of it stated here, because the control ships it. Three panes are refused as two affordances under one label, which is the slider's two-thumb sentence one screen on. A vertical arm is refused because the axis is the stylesheet's and not the row's, and a second arm proves nothing the accordion's did not while costing two more storyboard frames to keep true"
	"decision-41": "A sortable header ships, and what made it possible is that data-order stopped being one literal without becoming an interpolation. The refusal it replaces was sound: interpolating the clause would let a ROW name a COLUMN, which is reflection, and the set of reads a screen has would stop being enumerable — a reviewer could no longer finish reading what the screen can do. Interpolating a KEY of a map the file states costs none of that. Every order the grid can be read in is written down, a column picks among them, and a key the map does not carry is a ProgramError rather than an unordered read. It is the same trade data-when's arms and a machine's state set already make: vary over a closed set enumerated in the markup, never over a name. The header's controls are two commands per column rather than one toggle, which is shadcn's own header menu. A toggle would have to know which order it is already in and answer differently, and that is a conditional no binding states; two literals need no such answer, and pressing one twice states the same row. aria-sort is per column while the order is one fact, so each command states every sortable header's attribute as a literal and they move as one row — the #OneOf shape, in forms rather than a chart, since choosing an order is a write a form can name in advance. The other two rows of the closing pane's table are unchanged and stay refused: an aggregate is a read across rows and a region hands its template one, and a checkbox per row is a machine per row"
	"decision-42": "The tooltip ships on hover, and interestfor is still implemented in no engine — both are true, because the terminal performs the open. data-interest names the surface a trigger opens on hover or focus; the terminal holds the wait before it opens, the grace that lets a reader move onto it, and nothing else. It is NOT spelled interestfor: naming an attribute after a spec no engine ships would put a standard's clothes on our own behaviour, and a spec landing differently would strand the name. When it ships this deletes, and the rename is the whole migration. Three things keep it from being the imperative ad-hoc the doctrine names as the disease. The waits are the terminal's clock, so ?tempo= and ?clock=manual govern them like every other delay the platform performs — a hover delay on a timer of its own would be nondeterminism no replay could hold still. The surface is popover=\"auto\", which is load-bearing and not tidiness: light dismiss and Escape are WCAG 1.4.13's Dismissable clause and they come from the element, so this code never listens for a key. And it holds NO STATE, which is the licence — a surface no row decides cannot disagree with one, so nothing reaches the store, nothing is journalled, a replay has nothing to reproduce, and the overlays screen still counts zero regions and zero machines. That is the same licence popover=\"auto\"'s own light dismiss already runs on, and it is why this is not an escape hatch wearing a declaration. A surface has ONE owner. data-interest naming a surface whose openness is already a column is refused rather than arbitrated: the two would disagree the moment either moved — the row restating its answer on the next bind would shut a surface the reader is still under. It is a second component rather than a parameter on the first, because what opens a surface decides its ARIA all the way down. decision-14's tip opens on a CLICK and takes focus, so role=\"tooltip\" is a role it does not meet and aria-describedby would read its text out on every focus of the trigger — the announcement a disclosure exists to defer. This one opens on hover or focus and never takes focus, so it IS APG's tooltip and carries both. The CSS-only hover route stays refused for the reason it always was: it fails 1.4.13 by construction, and a reference that paints a known AA failure gets the failure copied"
	"decision-43": "Home and End ship on the palette, and what they cost was not the clause. first and last answer over the rows a region holds, so both ends are the ends of what the FILTER left — which is what a reader can see, and the only reading that makes sense for a list that narrows as they type. With nothing chosen, three of the four keys mean entering a list at the top and share the one limit=1 read that answers the head; End is the one that does not, so it takes its own read: the same filter and limit under the reverse order, whose single row is the last one. What actually stood in the way was a contract. Every key here names a form off the caret's own id, so a filter matching nothing renders no forms and the key names one that is not there — and data-key called that a program error, loudly, on a state a reader reaches by typing. Both this screen and the data table threw on it before this wave, and no test saw it: linkedom keeps a <template>'s children parented to the template, so its getElementById answered from markup that had never rendered, while a browser cannot reach that content at all. The harness isolates template content now, which is what turned the bug into something a test can hold. The rule that replaced it reads the DECLARATION rather than the resolved value. A literal id naming nothing is the markup naming a form that is not there, and stays a loud error — that is the typo case, and it is worth keeping. An INTERPOLATED one may legitimately resolve to nothing, because the set it names can be empty, so a miss there is a no-op and the key is left uncancelled: the arrow does what an arrow does when there is no list to walk. Nothing is written and nothing is said"
	"decision-44": "The one-time code is ONE control, and the boxes are the stylesheet's. shadcn's InputOTP is six slots with a caret crossing them, and this vocabulary can spell that exactly: data-focus plus a column naming the slot the reader is on, which is the accordion's caret with a different skin. It is refused, and the refusal is the component. Three things a one-time code exists for die at the seam between six controls. A paste of six digits is one input event carrying a whole value; six fields turn it into a distribution problem nothing in this vocabulary answers. autocomplete=\"one-time-code\" names a control, and the platform fills THAT control — six of them means the first box gets the code and the other five get nothing. And Backspace at the head of a box is a rule somebody writes per box, where one field has the browser's own. What one field does not hold is whether what has been typed IS a code, and that is the whole of the chart: three arrows on one event, asked in turn — a complete code, a code still being typed, and a keystroke that can never be part of one. The third has no guard, which is what makes it the answer for everything the two above declined; every one of them writes the column, because an arrow that declined to write would leave the control showing text the row has not got and no delta to correct it with. pattern=\"[0-9]{6}\" with :valid would style this with no chart at all, and it is refused for the reason the form screen turns the UA's own validation off: a fact only CSS knows is one no row holds, no test reads and no reviewer signs. Here it is a column, so disabled on the verify button IS that column and the verify arrow carries no guard — what makes the click unreachable is the same fact that makes the boxes green. Two leaves, and both are the rule decision-34 states rather than an exception to it: a transition grammar has no length, no character class and no comparison, so the predicate is a module the chart names and the number rides as a param — the same number the control's maxlength, the column's constraint and the stylesheet's track count are emitted from"
	"decision-45": "The hover card is a component of its own rather than the tip with a bigger body, because what is INSIDE a surface decides its ARIA — which is the same rule decision-42 used to split the two tips by what OPENS them. A tooltip's surface is a string, so it can be a description its trigger points at; a card's is a structure with its own links, so it can be neither. aria-describedby flattens what it names into one announcement and would read a card's whole layout out on every focus of the name, and role=\"tooltip\" promises a surface with nothing inside to reach. So this one carries neither, and the tip's pair is the contrast rather than an omission here. What makes the card reachable is DOCUMENT ORDER and nothing else: sequential focus navigation walks an open popover in tree order, so the surface following its trigger puts the card's link next in the tab sequence with no tabindex anywhere. Nothing in the markup arranges that — it is the element's, like the top layer and the light dismiss. It is why data-interest hears focus on the SURFACE and not only on the trigger. Tab takes focus off the trigger, so a surface hearing the pointer alone closes on the reader's way into it — taking their focus with it, since the element holding it is gone. The keyboard's pair is 1.4.13's HOVERABLE clause read for a keyboard, and it costs a tip nothing: a surface holding one string never receives focus, so those listeners can never fire there. No entity, no chart, no module. Openness stays nobody's row for decision-42's reason, and a surface has one owner — so this screen counts zero regions and zero store calls the way the overlays screen does, and the claim is a counted absence rather than a sentence"
	"decision-46": "The chart's geometry is a COLUMN reaching a stylesheet, and its domain is the PROGRAM's. Each bar carries style=\"--v: {value}\", the frame carries --chart-max, and a length is calc() over the two — so the markup does no arithmetic at all, which is decision-40's binding used for the thing it was introduced to do. The rule that binding shipped under was narrower than the mechanism: a column a READER can type into stays out of a stylesheet. These numbers are the program's own, so this screen is inside that rule rather than a widening of it. The scale is computed where the rows are stated — list.Max over the same list the seed comes from — so the domain and the data cannot drift. A scale derived at RUNTIME would be an aggregate, and this platform has two seats for one and no clause (2026-09-03's refusal): a browser-tier fold woken by a mutation, and #Pipeline's four pure functions at a server tier. It would also move every bar in the picture whenever one row arrived, which makes two readings of the same chart incomparable. Stating the domain is what a y-axis has always been. A LINE ships, and it is not a polyline: a polyline's points is one value over every row, so the line is a SEGMENT per row, which is a binding again. The only thing it needs that a bar does not is the NEXT row's value — a bar's length is its own row's number, and a segment reaches somewhere else. The projection names that neighbour and answers ids and positions rather than its columns, so the value is carried in a column of its own, which the program states because the program states the rows. Over rows a READER can change that column is a derived one somebody maintains, and the seats for that are a browser-tier fold woken by a mutation and #Pipeline's four pure functions at a server tier. The line is not waiting on a capability; it is waiting on whoever owns the rows. It is not SVG, and that is a platform fact rather than a preference: a <template>'s content is parsed as HTML, so a region filling an <svg> from one produces elements in the wrong namespace — in the tree, carrying every attribute the binding wrote, and drawn by no engine. linkedom renders that markup happily, which is what made the browser the place it was seen. So a segment is a box holding its own two numbers and the shape is a clip between them: the same calc() over a bound value and a bound domain the bars use, with the axis flipped by subtracting from 100% and no trigonometry, because the horizontal step IS the box. The line carries aria-hidden and no text: it draws the same rows the table does, so it is decoration over the data rather than a second copy of it. The picture is its own TABLE. A chart's accessible alternative is the data it draws, and shipping both means keeping two things in step — so the data ships and the stylesheet draws it: a row header per category, a cell per value, and the bar is that cell's own submit button, its accessible name carrying the category and the number it draws. What is seen is inside what is said, which is 2.5.3's own clause. The caret is decision-35's, over rows rather than over a compile-time item set: one projected answer decides the fill, the tab order and the readout together, so a bar cannot look current and not be. It is data-rove and not data-focus because a plot is one affordance in the Tab sequence, which is what APG gives every composite whose arrows move a selection — and the answer it binds is the projection's, not a column, which is the case the caret rules had never been shown and now judge: nothing writes a projected answer, so the tier question moves to the row the projection compares against. The bar being a submit button is what keeps one caret meaning one thing — a click writes the column the arrows write, and there is no second way to be current"
	"decision-47": "A control whose ARIA value is a FUNCTION of other state has no states of its own. decision-18 named two ways to hold that function and refused both — a machine whose states are the item subsets, at 2^N of them, and a cross-row read row closure forbids — and concluded that the items must ship as text. There is a third way, and it was already in this catalog: the move decision-23 made for a value with no states. The header's value stops being the machine's FIELD and becomes a column an arrow assigns off a leaf. No exponent and no second row. The leaf reads the same row the chart is closed over, which is the closure working rather than being worked around, and the arrow that flips an item assigns the header in the SAME write — so the two are never seen disagreeing, which a second region computing the header would not have been able to promise. What it costs is decision-03's reading, and only where the rule never applied: state names are the ARIA attribute's values for the facts a machine DECIDES. mixed is not one — it is what three items add up to — so it is a value, and the field beside it holds one state because there is nothing for this control to be in. The third value is reachable, which is the pattern working: a reader gets back to mixed by unchecking one of three, as often as they like. And the chart is SMALLER for it — one state, one arrow per affordance, not a guard among them — because what the items add up to is a computation and not a branch. Two leaves, both decision-34's rule rather than an exception to it. flip-of is the value a column takes next; roll-up is what the items add up to with the clicked one flipped. Neither is reachable except as a leaf the chart names. What this does NOT close is the case the aggregate refusal actually points at: items that are ROWS. Three columns of one row are a fold the closure can reach; a set the reader can grow is an aggregate over rows, and the seat for that is a fold woken by data-on-mutation — which this app's brief refuses, since a module reachable any other way than as a chart's leaf is a design failure it promises to surface. So the data table still has no select-all, and that is where the seat's first consumer belongs"
	"decision-48": "Three of these four components are SKINS over a fact something else already holds, and saying which is what the screen is for. A component that claims a fact the platform owns is one that can come to disagree with it — the same reasoning decision-42 used to refuse a surface whose openness was already a column. Empty is the sharpest. shadcn's is a component you place; here it is a STATE two things already have — a region renders its data-empty when its read answers nothing, and a route declares an empty state the terminal stamps on the screen. So the definition adds the skin and one thing a paragraph saying \"nothing here\" has not got: a way out, because empty with no action is a dead end a reader leaves through the browser's own controls. Spinner is the one that may not depend on its own animation. The brief holds every screen to collapsing under prefers-reduced-motion, so what survives the collapse has to carry the fact by itself: role=\"status\" and a label are the component, and the ring is decoration. Kbd is the element wearing the tokens, with one box per key — a key named Ctrl+K is a key nobody has, and a reader hears it that way. Typography adds nothing at all, and that is the entry: it is the design block's type scale, which every screen in this gallery already resolves against, so the specimen is a document and what it proves is that a screen needs no rule of its own to read correctly. The glyphs are drawn by the stylesheet rather than fetched: an icon font is a second asset and an <img> is a request, where a box a rule fills is neither — and this screen's whole stylesheet declares not one literal colour, which is what makes its dark frame the same declaration read the other way"
	"decision-49": "Three components that hold nothing, and each is exactly ONE relationship — which is what makes them worth expanding rather than writing out, because each relationship is one an expansion can quietly break. Item is which element the title is. A row that goes somewhere is a link; a row that does not is a div and NOT a link with no href, which is a control a keyboard cannot reach and a reader is told about anyway. The roster's fourth arrangement, Field, is the form screen's and predates the entry: a control, its label and its help text as one unit, with the three ids unified from one so the association cannot drift. A second definition here would be one relationship declared twice, and the screen names it rather than shipping it. What that Field does not carry is a per-control aria-invalid, and the reason is on that screen rather than missing from it: the verdict is checkValidity()'s, taken inside the shell's submit listener, so it is no column a binding could hold. Input group is a lie the stylesheet tells: the control's own border is gone and the group wears it, so the focus ring has to move there too — a ring left on the control leaves the visible edge and the focused thing two different boxes. Button group is a shared border over N INDEPENDENT actions, and the contract it can break is the one this catalog is most likely to break: a group whose buttons are one answer is decision-11's, and wears aria-checked and one tab stop. Wearing that ARIA over actions would promise a choice nothing here is making. Two of the four act, and the screen carries a chart for them alone: a one-state one (decision-23's shape), where what is typed is a value assigned off the event and what was pressed is a literal each arrow states. A screen of dead controls teaches a dead control. Direction ships as an attribute rather than as the roster's provider and hook. dir=\"rtl\" on any element mirrors what is inside it, because every edge this screen's rules name is logical — padding-inline, border-inline-start, text-align: start. Nothing in the terminal is told and no component changes. The claim is scoped to this screen on purpose: the rest of the catalog is unaudited, and a rule naming left is a rule the attribute cannot reach"
	"decision-50": "A transcript is ROWS and the kinds in it are a COLUMN. A marker is not a message with different styling — it is a row nobody said, so it carries no seat, no avatar and no bubble — and a file is a message with a state of its own. One region renders all three and the template branches on the column, which is what makes a fourth kind a value rather than a second region, the same move decision-35 makes for everything about a row that is not the row. The scroller is the one with behaviour, and its answer is a LAYOUT. flex-direction: column-reverse puts the scroll origin at the bottom: the newest row lands where the reader is already looking, and rows arriving above do not move them. The usual answer is a listener measuring scrollTop against scrollHeight, and this platform has no seat for one — which turns out not to matter for the clause that matters. It IS a tab stop, because a box that scrolls has to be reachable (decision-33's rule), and it carries role=\"log\" so a reader is told what arrives. What a layout cannot do is OPEN a saved thread where a reader left it: that is a measurement taken on the way out and restored on the way in, and neither end of it is a fact about a row. Named rather than approximated. The composer states EVERY column, because a row half stated is one the bindings read as blank — the seat, the kind, the names and the state ride as hidden literals it knows, and at is the terminal's clock, the one value neither the program nor the reader supplies. That is also why the row carries two columns for what looks like one fact: at orders the region and when is the time of day a reader reads, and no binding turns the first into the second. The attachment's states are a column like any other, so the shimmer is a rule keyed on it rather than an animation somebody starts. What this screen does not have is anything to upload WITH: blobs is a capability this app declares false, so the state is stated by the seed and the screen says so instead of animating a progress with nothing behind it"
	"decision-51": "The questionnaire's states are the SCREEN's steps rather than a control's, which is the first chart in this app shaped that way — and almost everything else about it is vocabulary the catalog already had. An answer is a column an arrow states as a literal (decision-11's shape, in a chart of the screen), an option's own aria-pressed is a column beside it because that attribute speaks true and false rather than the answer's words, and which question is showing is the field the panels project off — Radix's data-state contract, the same one the tabs keep. Progress is a RULE keyed on that field and not a column. A number computed from where you are is a second spelling of where you are, and two spellings drift; the bar is four rules, one per state, and there is nothing to keep in step. What a step adds is the block. Next is blocked until the question is answered, and the block is a COLUMN rather than a guard — the rule the one-time code ships under, followed here rather than deciding one fact twice. Skip is then exactly the arrow with no such column on it, which is what \"skippable\" means once the difference is written into a chart: it states the answer Skipped and goes on, so nothing downstream has to tell a skipped question from an unanswered one. Back is what makes the block interesting. Coming forward again must not ask a question already answered, so what a step arrow assigns is the DESTINATION's block, read off the row by a leaf. There are two leaves and not one, because the two SOURCES are two facts: a step arrow reads the row, and a keystroke reads the event, since the row still holds the answer before it. One leaf preferring the event would have broken every step arrow — each click carries a value, a button's own, which is the empty string — and it did, in a browser, while the shim tier passed: linkedom gives a button no value at all. The harness gives it one now. Three things it does not do, each with the reason rather than a deferral. A question that appears only after a particular answer is a guarded transition the grammar already has — what it costs is that the step set stops being the question set, and a reader of the chart can no longer see the path by reading its states. Resuming where a reader left off is the same chart over a device row, and the only thing that changes is one word in the program. And a count of the steps remaining is an aggregate over the chart's own STATES rather than over rows, so nothing can count them at runtime — the program can, where it states them"
}

